## F1 — behaviour 1/4's "argv is the policy" is blind to a repeated policy flag (FAIL)
talk-boundary.test.ts reads each logged argv with two first-occurrence helpers:
  flagsIn()     -> new Set(...)          duplicates collapse, so the flag-set equality passes
  valuesAfter() -> argv.indexOf(flag)    only the first group of values is judged
So a profile that emits the full correct policy and then appends a widening flag passes all four
behaviours. Mutation evidence (script kept below, each mutation restored, tree clean after):
  M5 resume: + "--allowedTools","Bash(bisellium *)"        exit=0  b1..b4 FAIL=0
  M6 POLICY_FLAGS + "--allowedTools","Bash(bisellium *)"    exit=0  b1..b4 FAIL=0
  M7 POLICY_FLAGS + "--tools","WebFetch"                    exit=0  b1..b4 FAIL=0
  M8 POLICY_FLAGS + "--permission-mode","acceptEdits"       exit=0  b1..b4 FAIL=0
These are real widenings on the installed vendor CLI (claude 2.1.252), captured from the
system/init event before the API call (no model turn):
  baseline argv                      -> permissionMode dontAsk      tools [Bash,Glob,Grep,Read]
  + --tools WebFetch                 -> permissionMode dontAsk      tools [Bash,Glob,Grep,Read,WebFetch]
  + --permission-mode acceptEdits    -> permissionMode acceptEdits  tools [Bash,Glob,Grep,Read]
Repeated --tools is unioned and repeated --permission-mode is last-wins. --allowedTools is the same
variadic option type as --tools, so M5/M6 are expected to union too. This is inferred: init does
not echo approval rules.
Why it blocks: the brief's own ruling names "a tick-specific profile or widening flag" as the
regression this opus must keep out, and an appended widening flag is exactly the one the suite
does not catch.
Class, all instances (searched every call site of flagsIn/valuesAfter in talk-boundary.test.ts):
  1. checkPolicy flag-set equality via flagsIn (behaviour 1 start/resume, behaviour 4 talk+tick)
  2. checkPolicy --permission-mode via valuesAfter (b1, b4)
  3. checkPolicy --tools via valuesAfter (b1, b4)
  4. checkPolicy --allowedTools via valuesAfter (b1, b4)
  5. behaviour 2's allowlist via valuesAfter(argv,"--allowedTools") (start, resume)
  6. behaviour 3's allowlist via the same call (start, resume)
One fix covers all six: each policy flag must occur exactly once. Alternatively, assert each form's
argv equals the literal expected array, with the temp prompt path and session id as the only
variables. Behaviours 2 and 3 should then judge the union of every --allowedTools group.
Source (claude-code.ts) is correct today: no duplicates. The defect is in the test's reach. The
brief's "the set of --flags" wording invited it, and the fix stays inside the owned test file. No
spec change is needed.

